IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- INSTRUMENT: Telecommunication Authority of Singapore (Exemption in respect of Specified Equipment) Order (Cap. 323, O 3), made by the Minister for Communications under section 130 of the Telecommunication Authority of Singapore Act (Cap. 323),
-- the "repealed Act" of the Telecommunications Act 1999.  As made: G.N. No. S 549/1998 (first published 11 November 1998 at 5:00 pm, in operation 12 November 1998).  Also deposited: the Order in the 1999 Revised Edition (1 July 1999).
-- Both under subjects/sg/telecommunications-tas-exemption-specified-equipment-order-1998/registers/source-bundle/ (TA1999-O3-S549-1998, TA1999-O3), the e-Gazette's copies, deposited 2026-11-04 from the follow-up list.
--
-- WHAT IT SAYS.  Paragraph 2: "All persons shall be exempted from section 30 (1) to (5) of the Act in respect of (a) any telecommunication equipment that has been approved for sale by the Authority under regulation 24 (1) of the Telecommunication (Dealers) Regulations (Rg 7);
-- and (b) any telecommunication equipment that is set out in the Fifth Schedule to the Telecommunication (Dealers) Regulations and that meets the standards and specifications approved by the Authority and published by the Authority from time to time."  The 1999 Revised Edition prints "any" before
-- "telecommunication equipment" in both limbs; S 549/1998 does not.  Rg 7 (1999 Ed.) reg 24(1) and its Fifth Schedule are in sgta-i-rg7-1999.l4.
--
-- IS IT STILL IN FORCE?  TA s 98(2) continues subsidiary legislation "made under the repealed Act ... and in force immediately before 1 December 1999 ... as if made under this Act until it is revoked or repealed by subsidiary legislation made under this Act" (TA1999.txt:7097-7102).
-- The 2010 Subject Index of Singapore's subsidiary legislation lists the Order, under the heading "equipment, telecommunication", beside the Telecommunications (Dealers) Regulations (Cap 323, Rg 6) (TA1999-AID-INDEX2010.txt:9717-9721, quoted below the header; an index as at 1 June 2010 that says nothing of later years, deposited as an AID and not as an instrument).
-- No revoking instrument has been found.  That is the result of a search, not proof: the team lead's search of the e-Gazette index for the main-series notices of 2010 to 2013 read the first page of results only (NOTES.md, source-not-obtained table), and the absence of a hit is not proof that none exists.
--
-- THE FORK THIS MODULE CARRIES.  The Order exempts from "section 30 (1) to (5) of the Act", a section of the TAS Act whose text is NOT held (source not obtained: the e-Gazette's index offers no text of that Act; lawplain lists it as "TASA1992" with no sections;
-- the Singapore Statutes Online refused the BA encoder's one attempt at robots.txt and this encoder made none).  What that section said decides what the Order reaches:
--   BRANCH 1 (the evidence in hand favours it): section 30(1) to (5) was the approval-before-use requirement, the predecessor of section 9 of the 2000 Edition of the Telecommunications Act.  The Order then says that equipment already approved for sale, or listed as not needing approval,
--     needs no separate approval before use, and it says nothing about possessing or operating equipment.  Extrinsic evidence: clause 6 of the Telecommunication Authority of Singapore (Amendment) Bill 1998 (read the first time on 14 January 1998, ten months before the Order was made)
--     would have amended "section 30(1) of the principal Act" by "deleting the words 'public telecommunication' and substituting the words 'telecommunication system'", which is the wording of section 9(1) of the 2000 Edition ("Any equipment to be used for connection to any telecommunication
--     system or equipment belonging to a telecommunication system licensee shall be approved by the Authority before use", TA1999.20001230.txt:510-512).  That is a Bill as introduced and shows a phrase, not the section as passed (extrinsic; lawplain `bills`, B6-1998, read by the independent reviewer, not re-read here).
--   BRANCH 2: section 30(1) to (5) was the prohibition on possessing or operating equipment.  Limb (a) would then exempt every person in respect of any equipment whose type was approved for sale under reg 24(1).
--   NEITHER is established by a text held.  The function below therefore answers NOTHING when the reading of section 30 is not supplied.
--
-- A TRAP IN THE REFERENCES.  The Order's "Fifth Schedule" is the Fifth Schedule of Rg 7 (1999 Ed.), "TELECOMMUNICATION EQUIPMENT NOT REQUIRING APPROVAL" (TA1999-DEAL99.txt, Fifth Schedule).  The counterpart in the Dealers Regulations (Rg 6), which replaced Rg 7, is its FIRST Schedule, "TELECOMMUNICATION EQUIPMENT NOT REQUIRING APPROVAL"
-- (TA1999-RG6.txt:413-420); Rg 6's Fifth Schedule is the fee table for tests on equipment for personal use (TA1999-RG6.txt:581-584).  A reader who takes "the Fifth Schedule" to mean Rg 6's is reading across two different schedules.
-- Section 15(2)(a) of the Interpretation Act 1965 reads a reference to a repealed and re-enacted provision "as a reference to the provision so re-enacted" (IA1965.txt:1052-1058, the current text); on that reading "regulation 24 (1)" of Rg 7 is reg 20(1) of Rg 6, whose words are the same (TA1999-RG6.txt:349-355).
-- This module does not decide how those references are carried; it encodes the Order's words against Rg 7 (1999 Ed.).
-- The seat modules (sgta-tv-*.l4) do not consult this module.

